-- title:
--   Naturality of the Ω̂-family on rigidified points (connected-injectivity edition)
-- statement:
--   The setting is the Čerednik–Drinfeld dictionary for fake elliptic curves, assembled from the following data and hypothesis groups.
--
--   *Arithmetic of the base.* Primes $r$ and $\bar r$ with $\bar r \neq r$, a nonzero level $N$ with $N$ squarefree and divisible by neither $r$ nor $\bar r$; a characteristic-zero discrete valuation domain $\mathcal O$ (hypothesis `hdvr`) with irreducible element $\pi$, complete for the $\pi$-adic topology, with residue ring $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ of cardinality $r$ and $(r) = (\pi)$ in $\mathcal O$; a characteristic-zero fraction field $K_0$ of $\mathcal O$. A characteristic-zero domain $Onr$ over $\mathcal O$ with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $Fr$, such that $Onr$ is complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$, that ideal is maximal, every element of $Onr$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo it (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial of positive degree over $Onr$ has a root modulo it (`hOnr_closed`), and $Fr(x) \equiv x^{r}$ modulo it for all $x$ (`hFr`). A homomorphism $vdet : GL_2(K_0) \to \mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) characterised by `hvdet`: $vdet(g) = n$ if and only if $\det g = u\pi^{n}$ for some unit $u$ of $\mathcal O$.
--
--   *Quaternionic data and moduli.* Rationals $a,b$ with `hB` asserting that $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ is indefinite ($0<a$ or $0<b$) and, at each finite place $v$, is a division algebra exactly when $v$ divides $r$ or $\bar r$; a maximal order $\Lambda$ in it. A scheme $\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ with a point map $pt$ on fake elliptic curves of level $N$, satisfying the coarse-moduli axioms `h𝒳` (isomorphism invariance, compatibility with pull-back, surjectivity and injectivity on geometric points, and the universal property). An integer $n \geq 3$ coprime to $N$ and divisible by neither $r$ nor $\bar r$, a scheme $M$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ with point map $ptF$ on fake elliptic curves with full level $n$ satisfying the fine-moduli axioms `hM`, a group $G$ acting on $M$ by $\rho$ with labels $\chi : G \to \Lambda$ satisfying the level-twist axioms `hρ`, and a morphism $p : M \to \mathcal X$ over $\mathcal O$ (`hp`), invariant under the $G$-action (`hρp`) and compatible with the two point maps (`hp_pt`). For each prime $\ell$ distinct from $r$ and $\bar r$, a scheme $\mathcal Y_\ell$ over $\mathcal O$ with point map $ptT$ on curves with extra level $\ell$ satisfying `h𝒴`, together with degeneracy morphisms $d_0,d_1 : \mathcal Y_\ell \to \mathcal X$ over $\mathcal O$ (`hd₀f`, `hd₁f`) such that $d_0$ forgets the extra level (`hd₀`) and $d_1$ records the $\ell$-isogenous quotient (`hd₁`), and Atkin–Lehner endomorphisms $a_r, a_{\bar r}$ of $\mathcal X$ over $\mathcal O$ realising the respective Atkin–Lehner quotients on points (`har`, `harbar`).
--
--   *Definite quaternion side.* Rationals $a_1,b_1$ with `hdef` asserting that $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ is definite ($a_1<0$, $b_1<0$) and ramified exactly at $\bar r$; a maximal order $\Lambda_1$, an Eichler order $R_1$ of level $N$ contained in $\Lambda_1$, an idèle $n_1$ in the $r$-prime Hecke set of $R_1$ such that the intersected order $R_1 \cap n_1 R_1 n_1^{-1}$ is Eichler of level $Nr$; an injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra embedding $\iota_0$ of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ into $M_2(K_0)$, and a finite place $v$ containing $r$. The subgroup $\Gamma_t$ of units is characterised by `hΓt` as the away-from-$v$ unit group of $R_1$, together with elements $s_\ell$ and idèles $sf_\ell$ indexed by primes $\ell \neq r, \bar r$ satisfying `hs`: $sf_\ell$ has component $s_\ell$ at every place not containing $r$, component $1$ at places containing $r$, the product of the diagonal idèle of $\ell$ with $sf_\ell^{-1}$ lies in the level Hecke set (when $\ell \mid N$) or the prime Hecke set (otherwise) of the intersected order, and $\mathrm{nrd}(s_\ell) = \ell$; the groups $\Gamma_{t,\ell} = \Gamma_t \cap s_\ell \Gamma_t s_\ell^{-1}$ (`hΓtℓ`); and a unit $wbar$ of reduced norm $\bar r$ normalising $\Gamma_t$ (`hwbar`).
--
--   *Formal data at $r$.* The hypothesis `hΛℤ` that every rational integer lies in $\Lambda$, and a coordinate map $coord : \Lambda \to Zp2(r)\times Zp2(r)$ satisfying the order-coordinate axioms `hcoord` (additivity, normalisation, the twisted multiplication rule, injectivity, density and the trace condition). A fake elliptic curve $A_0$ over the residue ring $Onr/(\pi)$, a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $X_0$ over that ring of height $4$ (`hX₀`), formal coordinates $\theta_0$ of $A_0$ in two variables with `hA₀` asserting that $\theta_0$ identifies the formal group of $A_0$ with $X_0$ compatibly with $coord$, and families $e,e' : \Gamma_t \to \operatorname{End}(A_0.A)$ over the base (`he`) with degrees $deg$.
--
--   *The uniformisation axioms for $A_0$.* `hE1`: each $e(\gamma), e'(\gamma)$ is an isogeny pair of degree $r^{\deg\gamma}$ preserving the level structure. `hE1mul`: multiplicativity of $\gamma \mapsto e(\gamma)$ up to $r$-power multiplications. `hE1sc`: scalars $\gamma = c$ act as multiplication by $c$, up to $r$-powers. `hE1'`: conversely, if $e(\gamma)$ becomes a positive integer multiplication after an $r$-power, then $\gamma$ is a rational scalar. `hE2`: for every injective ring homomorphism $E_0$ from the centraliser of the $Zp2$-action and the uniformiser endomorphism of $X_0$ into $M_2(K_0)$ there is $g \in GL_2(K_0)$ such that each $\gamma \in \Gamma_t$ is matched by an element $\varepsilon$ of that centraliser which induces $e(\gamma)$ through $\theta_0$ on all nilpotent test data and whose image under $E_0$ is $r^{k_\gamma} g\,\iota_0(\gamma)\,g^{-1}$. `hE3`: every full level-$n$ structure $P_0$ on $A_0$ admits labels $lab : \Gamma_t \to \Lambda$ describing the action of $e(\gamma)$ on $P_0$, multiplicative modulo $n$ and compatible with scalars modulo $n$. `hE4`: for any algebraically closed field $k$ over the residue ring and any fake elliptic curve $A$ over $k$ pulled back from $A_0$, every $r$-power-degree isogeny pair of $A$ preserving the level comes from some $e(\gamma)$ up to $r$-powers. The group `hE5` of hypotheses concerns a further order $R_2 \leq R_1$ (an order, `hR₂o`) which absorbs $R_1$ after $r$-powers (`hR₂r`) and a family $\hat e : R_2 \to \operatorname{End}(A_0.A)$ over the base (`hê`): `hE5a` ($\hat e(x)$ is additive, commutes with the $\Lambda$-action and preserves the level), `hE5b1`–`hE5b3` (unitality, multiplicativity, and agreement with multiplication by integers), `hE5c` ($\hat e(\bar x)\hat e(x)$ is multiplication by $\mathrm{nrd}(x)$), `hE5d` (compatibility of $\hat e$ with $e$ on $r$-power multiples of elements of $\Gamma_t$, up to $r$-powers), and `hE5e` (the statement of `hE2` with one matrix $g$ serving both $\Gamma_t$ and $R_2$).
--
--   *Drinfeld's special formal functor and its comparison.* A ring homomorphism $\iota : Zp2(r) \to Onr$; a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $\Phi$ over $Onr/(r)$ which is special with respect to $\iota$ (`hΦ`) and of height $4$ (`hΦ4`); a moduli package $MD$ over $Onr$ which is a Zariski sheaf (`hMD`); a map $\eta$ from rigidified objects to $MD$ satisfying `hη`, namely that on Noetherian test rings $\eta$ is injective on admissible rigidified objects up to isomorphism, natural in ring maps, and locally surjective for the Zariski topology. A homomorphism $E_0$ from the centraliser of the $Zp2$-action and uniformiser endomorphism of $\Phi$ into $M_2(K_0)$ with `hE₀`: $E_0$ is injective and, for some $m$, the image of $E_0$ is $r^{-m}$ times the integral matrices in the two senses stated. A comparison family $eD$ from the Grothendieck-style functor $\mathcal G =$ `ModuliPackage.G 𝒪 MD` to the product of the functor corepresented by $Onr$ and Drinfeld's formal upper half-plane functor $\Omega_{K_0,\pi}$, defined on $\pi$-nilpotent Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebras, subject to: naturality (`hnatD`), bijectivity (`hbijD`), compatibility with the $Onr$-leg (`hfstD`), translation of the $GL_2(K_0)$-action into the twisted action of `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct` relative to $\pi$, $Onr$, $Fr$, $vdet$ (`hGLD`), and translation of $\pi$-translation into the Frobenius twist of the leg with unchanged Deligne datum (`hPiD`), together with the existence of $\pi$-translates (`hPiexD`).
--
--   *Transport between $X_0$ and $\Phi$.* A homomorphism $\kappa : Onr/(\pi) \to Onr/(r)$ compatible with the quotient maps (`hκ`), an integer $n_0$ and a series $\beta_0$ which is an isogeny of height $4n_0$ from $\Phi$ to the reduction of $X_0$ along $\kappa$ (`hβ₀`). A full level-$n$ structure $P_0$ on $A_0$.
--
--   *Atkin–Lehner data at $\bar r$ on $A_0$.* A fake elliptic curve $A_{0w}$ over the residue ring together with morphisms $aw, aw', bw, bw'$ over the base as indicated, an integer $kw$, such that $(aw,aw')$ exhibits $A_{0w}$ as the Atkin–Lehner $\bar r$-quotient of $A_0$ (`hALw`), $(bw,bw')$ is an isogeny pair of degree $r^{kw}$ from $A_{0w}$ to $A_0$ preserving the level (`hBSw`, `hBSwlev`), and `hJOINTw`: for every injective $E_0$ as above there is a single $g \in GL_2(K_0)$ matching, in the sense of `hE2`, every $\gamma \in \Gamma_t$ with $\iota_0(\gamma)$ and additionally the composite $aw$ followed by $bw$ with $\iota_0(wbar)$, up to $r$-powers.
--
--   *The dictionary $\Xi$.* A map $\Xi$ sending, for each Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent and each $Onr$-leg $\psi$, a rigidified fake elliptic curve over $(B,\psi)$ to a point of $\mathcal G$ over $B$, subject to: `hΞleg` (over connected $B$, the leg of $\Xi$ is a Frobenius twist $Fr^{k}$ of $\psi$), `hΞnat` (compatibility with pull-back of rigidified curves along $\mathcal O$-algebra maps), `hΞiso` (invariance under isomorphisms of rigidified curves in the sense spelled out: an isomorphism of the underlying curves compatible with the group law, the $\Lambda$-action and the level, together with comparison maps of the reductions and of the auxiliary curves matching the isogenies up to a pair of $r$-power multiplications), and `hΞdef` (over connected $B$ with $r$ nilpotent, $\Xi$ is computed by $\eta$: for a rigidified curve whose formal group is identified with a formal $\mathcal O_D$-module $X$ via $\theta$ there are $j \leq 1$ and an admissible rigidified object $t$ with $t.X = X$ transporting $\theta_0,\kappa,\beta_0$ to $\theta$ in $j$ steps, such that $\Xi$ of the curve equals the point of $\mathcal G$ with leg $Fr^{-j}\psi$ and component $\eta(t)$).
--
--   *Equivariance of $\Xi$.* An element $g_0 \in GL_2(K_0)$ such that `heqΓ`: over connected Noetherian $\pi$-nilpotent $B$, if two rigidifications $\rho,\rho'$ of the same curve $E$ are related by translation by $e(\gamma)$, then for some $c \in \mathbb Z$ the points $\Xi(E,\rho)$ and $\Xi(E,\rho')$ are related by the action of $r^{c} \cdot g_0\iota_0(\gamma)g_0^{-1}$ in the sense of `ModuliPackage.G.IsActBy`; and `heqW`: over such $B$ with $\bar r$ invertible, if $(q,q')$ exhibits $E_f$ as the Atkin–Lehner $\bar r$-quotient of $E$, and rigidifications $\rho$ of $E$ and $\rho_f$ of $E_f$ are related by the comparison data displayed (maps of reductions and auxiliary curves, an endomorphism $ewb$ over $aw$ followed by $bw$, matching the isogenies up to $r$-powers), then $\Xi(E,\rho)$ and $\Xi(E_f,\rho_f)$ are related by the action of $r^{c} \cdot g_0\iota_0(wbar)g_0^{-1}$ for some $c \in \mathbb Z$.
--
--   *The abstract point functor.* A Noetherian $\mathcal O$-algebra $C$ with $\pi$ nilpotent (`hCπ`) and an $Onr$-leg $\chi_C : Onr \to C$; a functor $PR$ on $C$-algebras; and a point map $ptR$ assigning, to each $C$-algebra $S$ which is also an $\mathcal O$-algebra compatibly, each leg $\psi_S : Onr \to S$ equal to $\chi_C$ followed by $C \to S$, each fake elliptic curve $u$ with full level $n$ over $S$ and each rigidification $\rho$ of $u$ relative to $A_0$ and $\psi_S$, an element $ptR(S,\psi_S,u,\rho)$ of $PR(S)$. Three axioms are imposed: `hR2`, that $ptR$ is compatible with pull-back — for a $C$-algebra map $\varphi : S \to S'$, curves $u,u'$ with rigidifications $\rho,\rho'$ and a pull-back morphism $g$ of the underlying curves carrying the level structure of $u'$ to that of $u$ and the rigidification $\rho$ to $\rho'$, one has $PR(\varphi)(ptR(S,\psi_S,u,\rho)) = ptR(S',\varphi\circ\psi_S,u',\rho')$; `hR3s`, that every element of $PR(S)$ is of the form $ptR(S,\psi_S,u,\rho)$; and `hR3i`, injectivity over connected bases — if $S$ has no idempotents other than $0$ and $1$ and $ptR(S,\psi_S,u,\rho) = ptR(S,\psi_S,u',\rho')$, then there is an isomorphism $i$ of the underlying curves over $S$ compatible with the group law, the $\Lambda$-action, the level subscheme and the full level-$n$ point, together with comparison maps $ib$ of the reductions and $uA$ of the auxiliary curves and exponents $i_1,j_1$ making the isogenies of $\rho$ and $\rho'$ agree after multiplication by $r^{i_1}$ and $r^{j_1}$.
--
--   Under these hypotheses the conclusion asserts the existence of a family
--   $$\theta_S : PR(S) \longrightarrow \Omega_{K_0,\pi}(S),$$
--   defined for every Noetherian $C$-algebra $S$ which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra compatibly with the $C$-structure, with the following two properties.
--
--   First, naturality: for all such $S, S'$, every $C$-algebra homomorphism $g : S \to S'$ and every $x \in PR(S)$,
--   $$\theta_{S'}(PR(g)(x)) = \Omega_{K_0,\pi}(g)(\theta_S(x)),$$
--   where on the right $g$ is regarded as an $\mathcal O$-algebra map.
--
--   Second, computation on points: for every such $S$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent, every leg $\psi_S : Onr \to S$ equal to $\chi_C$ followed by $C \to S$, every fake elliptic curve $u$ with full level $n$ over $S$ and every rigidification $\rho$ of $u$ relative to $A_0$ and $\psi_S$,
--   $$\theta_S(ptR(S,\psi_S,u,\rho)) = \big(eD(S)(\Xi(S,\psi_S,(u,\rho)))\big)_2 \text{ pulled back along } (g_0^{-1})^{-1},$$
--   that is, the second component of the comparison $eD$ applied to $\Xi$ of the rigidified curve, transported by `DeligneDatum.pullback` along the matrix $(g_0^{-1})^{-1}$.
--
--   This is the construction of the Drinfeld-period map on the functor of rigidified fake elliptic curves with full level $n$: the assignment $(u,\rho) \mapsto$ (Deligne datum of $\Xi(u,\rho)$, conjugated by $g_0$) is shown to descend to a well-defined natural transformation from the abstract point functor $PR$ to Drinfeld's formal upper half-plane functor. It is the representability-agnostic form of the $\theta$-family, stated for an arbitrary point functor rather than a representing scheme, and it is used in the construction and uniqueness statements for the rigidified pairs occurring in the relative representability step of the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation.
